WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.039
Okay, so what is cumulative analysis?

2
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:09.720
It is aggregating the data progressively over the time.

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.560
And this is a very important technique in order to understand how our business is growing over time.

4
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:20.239
So how our business is progressing over time, whether it is growing or declining.

5
00:00:20.239 --> 00:00:22.340
It is a very interesting analysis.

6
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.399
So the formula is going to be very similar to the changes over time.

7
00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:29.319
But instead of having simple aggregations on the measure, we can aggregate our measure

8
00:00:29.319 --> 00:00:31.040
but this time cumulative.

9
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.560
So we are like adding stuff on top of each other's.

10
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:39.680
And the data again can split it by the date dimension because we want to track the progress over the time.

11
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.759
For example, we can find the running total of sales or the moving average of sales by a month.

12
00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:49.520
So now let's have again our simple example where our sales is split by the years.

13
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.599
Now this is the classic change over time, but in order now to make it cumulative, what can happen?

14
00:00:54.599 --> 00:00:56.520
We're going to take the measure and add to it.

15
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.119
For example, 2024, we have 300.

16
00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:05.720
And now for 2025, we're going to add the 300 together with the 100 in order to make it cumulative.

17
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.480
So for 2025, we're going to have 400.

18
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.459
And the same thing for 2026, we're going to go and add the 400 together with the 200.

19
00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:15.139
And with that, we will get 600.

20
00:01:15.139 --> 00:01:19.120
So as you can see, we are keep adding the values in order to generate something called

21
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.480
cumulative value.

22
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.080
Now for this type of analysis, we use in SQL the aggregate window functions in order to

23
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.120
find out the cumulative values.

24
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.519
So now let's go and apply our formula in order to find whether our business is growing or declining. So let's go.

25
00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:34.519
Okay, so now we have to analyze the following.

26
00:01:34.519 --> 00:01:39.239
We're going to calculate the total sales for each month and as well the running total of

27
00:01:39.239 --> 00:01:42.040
sales over time in order to analyze the trends.

28
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.720
So let's see how we're going to do that.

29
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.760
Let's start with the easy stuff where we're going to calculate the total sales for each month.

30
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.559
So we are calculating the changes over time and we have already done that.

31
00:01:50.559 --> 00:01:53.000
So all what we need is a date and a measure.

32
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.879
Our date is going to be the order date and the measure is going to be the sales amount

33
00:01:56.879 --> 00:01:59.360
from our fact table.

34
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.360
So let's create this.

35
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.160
And now we want to find the total sales for each month.

36
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.360
That means we're going to change the granularity of the order date from a day to a month.

37
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.080
And I usually like using the date rank for this kind of tasks and the granularity is

38
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.119
going to be the month.

39
00:02:13.119 --> 00:02:16.399
So this is the order date.

40
00:02:16.399 --> 00:02:21.039
And now for the sales, we're going to use aggregate functions, some sales as total sales.

41
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.039
And of course, we have to go and group up the data by the date.

42
00:02:26.039 --> 00:02:29.440
So let's go and execute it.

43
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.000
So as you can see, we have now the total sales for each month and don't forget to get rid of the nulls.

44
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.039
So we're going to say where order date is not null.

45
00:02:39.039 --> 00:02:40.279
Now it looks better.

46
00:02:40.279 --> 00:02:41.279
We don't have nulls.

47
00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:44.360
And of course, we can go and order the data by our date.

48
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.919
Now our measure is just aggregated for each month individually, right?

49
00:02:48.919 --> 00:02:49.960
But we don't want that.

50
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.199
We want to have like a running total.

51
00:02:52.199 --> 00:02:54.779
So we'd like to have like cumulative metric.

52
00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:57.279
In order to do that, we have to use window function.

53
00:02:57.279 --> 00:02:59.039
So let's go and do that.

54
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:02.800
We will use a sub query for that in order just to make it simple.

55
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.800
So what do we need?

56
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:04.800
We need the order date.

57
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.880
And let's say the total sales.

58
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.759
And here we have to have our window function.

59
00:03:10.759 --> 00:03:14.279
Then we're going to put the rest in a sub query.

60
00:03:14.279 --> 00:03:18.399
And of course, we can go and get rid of the order by because anyway, our data can be sorted

61
00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:19.559
using the window function.

62
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.240
So now let's start writing our window function.

63
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.399
We will have the sum of total sales.

64
00:03:26.399 --> 00:03:31.600
So we want to summarize those new values and you're going to build a window function like this over.

65
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.399
We don't have to go and partition anything.

66
00:03:33.399 --> 00:03:38.919
So we can go immediately and say order by our new order date that we have calculated.

67
00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:40.559
And we want it to be ascending.

68
00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:42.160
So actually, that's it.

69
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.600
So as running total sales.

70
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.880
So let's try that out.

71
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.520
So now if you look to the result, you can see that all those values are cumulative and

72
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.800
it is working like this.

73
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.720
The first total sales is equal to the total sales because previously we don't have anything.

74
00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.440
Now for the next row, what can happen is SQL is going to go and add this value to the previous one.

75
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.520
And with that, we get the running total value.

76
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:11.039
Now moving on to the third row, SQL is going to go and add all those three values together.

77
00:04:11.039 --> 00:04:14.940
And of course, this is going to give us the running total for this month and so on.

78
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.820
So as SQL is moving through the window, it is always adding the current value to all

79
00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.179
previous values.

80
00:04:22.179 --> 00:04:25.059
And this is because of the default frame of the window.

81
00:04:25.059 --> 00:04:29.839
The frame is going to be between the unbounded preceding and the current row.

82
00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:33.739
So that means, for example, if we are at this row over here, current total sales for this

83
00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:39.760
month is this one and the unbounded preceding is all the values before this month.

84
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.420
So that means we are getting all the previous values together with the current value.

85
00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.140
And with that, we will get the effect of the running total sales.

86
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.420
And of course, as you can see, it is going through all the years, right?

87
00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.359
Now we can go and limit the running total for only one year.

88
00:04:56.359 --> 00:05:00.519
So for each new year, it has to reset and start from scratch.

89
00:05:00.519 --> 00:05:02.820
So that means we are partitioning the data.

90
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.899
For each year, we would like to have partition.

91
00:05:04.899 --> 00:05:08.380
For the first year, it's going to be 2010. It is one row.

92
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:11.880
And for the 2011, we're going to get the whole partition over here.

93
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.320
So in order to partition our window, it's very simple.

94
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.559
We're going to go and say, partition by the order date. That's it.

95
00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:21.559
Let's go and execute it.

96
00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:24.359
Now let's go and check for the first partition for 2010.

97
00:05:24.359 --> 00:05:27.700
You can see the running total is the same as the first month.

98
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.760
But since we have only one month, that's it for this year.

99
00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:34.440
Now as we go to the next year, as you can see, it resets.

100
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.239
So you can see the running total sales for 2011.

101
00:05:37.239 --> 00:05:39.480
It is exactly as January.

102
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.799
It is not adding up now the value of the current value with the previous one, because the previous

103
00:05:44.799 --> 00:05:46.799
one is outside of the window.

104
00:05:46.799 --> 00:05:50.359
So as you can see, we are getting a running total for the whole year.

105
00:05:50.359 --> 00:05:53.440
And once we hit a new year, it is going to reset.

106
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.899
So it is working.

107
00:05:54.899 --> 00:05:57.839
And this is how we can create community values in SQL.

108
00:05:57.839 --> 00:06:02.000
And of course, if you would like to change the granularity of our data, it is very simple.

109
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.720
All you have to do is to go over here and say, instead of month, we're going to make it as a year.

110
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.959
And of course, don't forget to change as well the group by.

111
00:06:09.959 --> 00:06:11.880
So let's go and execute.

112
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.679
And with that, we are creating community values for each year.

113
00:06:15.679 --> 00:06:19.359
But of course, it makes no sense to partition by the years.

114
00:06:19.359 --> 00:06:21.899
Let's go and remove it and execute it again.

115
00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:27.679
And with that, you are creating the running total sales, the cumulative metric over the years.

116
00:06:27.679 --> 00:06:29.019
So as you can see, it is very simple.

117
00:06:29.019 --> 00:06:33.320
Now we can go and add like another measure and another aggregation.

118
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:38.100
Like for example, instead of finding the running total, we can find the moving average.

119
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.339
So let's, for example, go and get the moving average of the price.

120
00:06:42.339 --> 00:06:46.739
So first, we have to calculate the average of the price as average price.

121
00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:51.279
And now what we have to do is to go and make another window function over here where we

122
00:06:51.279 --> 00:07:00.600
are saying average, the average price, and we're going to go and call it moving average. That's it.

123
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.040
So let's go and execute it.

124
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.640
And with that, you are getting the moving average price of our sales. All right.

125
00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.600
So now you might still asking what is really different between using a normal aggregation

126
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.480
and cumulative aggregation?

127
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.839
Well, we usually use normal aggregations in order to check the performance of each individual row.

128
00:07:18.839 --> 00:07:23.559
Like if I want to see how each year is performing, I'm going to go and do a normal aggregation.

129
00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:28.359
But if you want to see a progression and you want to understand how your business is growing,

130
00:07:28.359 --> 00:07:33.440
you have to go and use cumulative aggregations because you can see easily here the progress

131
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.720
of your business over the years.

132
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:41.200
So there is like a difference between using cumulative value and normal aggregation. All right.

133
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.760
So with that, we have done with the cumulative analysis and you have learned all different

134
00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.260
types of aggregations.

135
00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.119
Now the next step in our roadmap, we're going to do performance analysis.

